#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119504 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36986a18-172c-5983-8f6f-3211b6e8f89d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/id #uuid "1f374865-c7f7-510c-b361-fbad352d86c6" :question/math-academy-id "e-3452" :question/problem "Find the radius of curvature for the curve $\\mathbf r(t) = t \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin t\\, \\mathbf j$ at $t = \\dfrac \\pi 2$." :question/worked-solution "The curvature $\\kappa(t)$ of a plane curve\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = x(t) \\, \\mathbf i + y(t)\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\kappa (t) = \\dfrac {\\mid x'(t) y''(t) - y'(t) x''(t) \\mid} {\\big([x'(t)]^2 + [y'(t)]^2\\big)^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n\n$$\nR(t) = \\dfrac 1 {\\kappa(t)}\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = t, \\qquad y(t) = \\sin t\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x^{′}(t) &= 1, & y^{′}(t)|= \\cos t \\\\ x^{″}(t)|= 0, & y^{″}(t)|=-\\sin t\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nκ(t) &= (\\mid 1 \\cdot (-\\sin t) - \\cos t \\cdot 0\\begin{vmatrix})/((1^{2} + (\\cos t)^{2})^{3/2}) \\\\ = (\\mid \\sin t\\end{vmatrix})/((1 + \\cos^{2} t)^{3/2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at\n\n$$\nt = \\dfrac{\\pi}2\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nκ(\\frac{π}{2}) &= (\\mid \\sin (\\frac{π}{2}) \\mid)/((1 + \\cos^{2} (\\frac{π}{2}))^{3/2}) \\\\\n&= (1)/((1 + 0^{2})^{3/2}) \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at\n\n$$\nt = \\dfrac{\\pi}2\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(\\frac{π}{2}) &= (1)/(κ(\\frac{π}{2})) = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119505 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "384072a8-f008-5279-bb58-8086eb42b710" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Cartesian Form"}] :question/id #uuid "2ff129e7-a923-5510-a35b-a6be403152ac" :question/math-academy-id "e-3453" :question/problem "Find the curvature of the curve $y = x^3$ at $x=-1$." :question/worked-solution "The curvature $\\kappa(x)$ of a plane curve $y = y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\kappa(x) = \\dfrac {\\mid y''(x) \\mid} {(1+[y'(x)]^2)^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $y'(x)$ and $y''(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′}(x) &= 3x^{2}, \\mid y^{″}(x)|= 6x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nκ(x) &= (\\mid 6x\\begin{vmatrix})/([1 + (3x^{2})^{2}]^{3/2}) \\\\ = (\\mid 6x\\end{vmatrix})/((1 + 9x^{4})^{3/2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nκ(-1) &= (\\mid 6(-1)\\begin{vmatrix})/((1 + 9(-1)^{4})^{3/2}) \\\\ = (\\mid - 6\\end{vmatrix})/((1 + 9)^{3/2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{6}{10^{3/2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{6}{10\\sqrt{10}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{50}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119506 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "814655d8-f459-5df5-b722-3024b87065db" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/id #uuid "d8e470ad-6a9a-5a03-a90e-b21b24cb6862" :question/math-academy-id "e-3454" :question/problem "The signed curvature of the plane curve $\\mathbf r(t) = \\big\\langle t, \\: \\sin t \\big\\rangle$ is given by\n$k (t) = \\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}}{\\sqrt{(1+\\cos^2 t)^3}}, \\quad t\\neq 0$.\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "The signed curvature $k(t)$ of a plane curve\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\left \\langle x(t), y(t) \\right \\rangle\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\nk (t) = \\dfrac {x'(t) y''(t) - y'(t) x''(t)} {\\big([x'(t)]^2 + [y'(t)]^2\\big)^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = t, \\qquad y(t) = \\sin t\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x^{′}(t) &= 1, & y^{′}(t)|= \\cos t \\\\ x^{″}(t)|= 0, & y^{″}(t)|=-\\sin t\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk(t) &= (1 \\cdot (-\\sin t) - 0 \\cdot \\cos t)/((1^{2} + (\\cos t)^{2})^{3/2}) \\\\\n&= (-\\sin t)/((1 + \\cos^{2} t)^{3/2}) \\\\\n&= (-sin ⁡ t)/(\\sqrt{(1 + \\cos^{2} t)^{3}})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120280 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "384072a8-f008-5279-bb58-8086eb42b710" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a978cfa-f3b8-56ed-a97f-287824704fe6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118156} {:answer/id #uuid "b4f69f18-5b4e-5c58-8147-8f6771ce39ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118157} {:answer/id #uuid "fd793ef2-0cb9-5d2a-b159-006ba49b52b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118158} {:answer/id #uuid "1217ee08-02cc-5c26-8704-9147c30becfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118159} {:answer/id #uuid "710c75d0-ca77-57a8-adbd-3d60ddd2bdd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a978cfa-f3b8-56ed-a97f-287824704fe6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118156} :answer-field/id #uuid "a611cfc3-eca3-5620-9f5a-061b630587c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118155}] :q